        "reflection": "Solomon's famous judgment between the two mothers isn't really a story about wisdom — it's a story about what genuine love is willing to do. The real mother's willingness to give up her son rather than watch him die is the very thing that exposes her. Her love isn't possessive. It doesn't insist on itself. It would rather lose what it loves most than see it destroyed. Solomon recognizes it instantly — only a real mother's love looks like that. I don't want you to miss the line this passage draws: the difference between love that serves the lover and love that serves the beloved. Possessive love holds on because letting go hurts too much. Sacrificial love releases because holding on would do harm. Every mother eventually faces some version of this — a moment where what's actually best for your child costs you something real: comfort, control, presence, the relationship you'd pictured. Real love, the kind Solomon spotted, is willing to pay it. And it usually isn't dramatic martyrdom. It's the daily, small choices to serve your child's real good instead of your own need to feel needed, or secure, or right.",
